  "final_answer": "I now have all the information needed about articles with countable and uncountable nouns. Let me compile the comprehensive answer.\n\n# Articles in English Grammar (Cambridge Dictionary)\n\n## Overview\nArticles (**a**, **an**, **the**) are a type of determiner that go before a noun.\n\n## Indefinite Article: A/AN\nShows that what is referred to is **not already known** to the speaker/listener (it is the indefinite article).\n- **Example:** *\"Do you have a car?\"*\n\n## Definite Article: THE\nShows that what is referred to is **already known** to the speaker/listener (it is the definite article).\n- **Example:** *\"Where did we park the car?\"*\n\n### Comparison Examples:\n| Indefinite (a/an) | Definite (the) |\n|---|---|\n| \"Have you been to an ice rink?\" | \"Have you been to the ice rink?\" |\n| \"Would you like an apple?\" | \"Would you like to try the apple pie?\" |\n| \"Do you have a cat?\" | \"Have you seen the cat?\" |\n\n## Pronunciation Rules\n- **\"a\"** is pronounced /ə/ before consonant sounds (e.g., *a car, a house, a big truck, a wheel, a grey day, a united group, a university, a one-year-old child*)\n- **\"an\"** is pronounced /ən/ before vowel sounds (e.g., *an apple, an old shoe, an orchestra, an umbrella, an hour, an MP3 player*)\n- **\"the\"** is pronounced /ðiː/ before vowel sounds (*the exit, the apple*)\n- **\"the\"** is pronounced /ðə/ before consonant sounds (*the team, the union*)\n\n## Articles with Types of Nouns\n\n### COUNTABLE NOUNS\n**A/AN** is used only with **singular** countable nouns:\n- *\"I have a sister and a brother.\"*\n- *\"That was an excellent meal.\"*\n\n**THE** can be used with **both singular and plural** countable nouns:\n- *\"The lion roared.\"* (singular) / *\"The lions roared.\"* (plural)\n- *\"The tree fell.\"* (singular) / *\"The trees fell.\"* (plural)\n\n### UNCOUNTABLE NOUNS\n**A/AN** is **NOT used** with uncountable nouns:\n- ✅ *\"I hope we have nice weather.\"*\n- ❌ ~~*\"I hope we have a nice weather.\"*~~\n\n**THE** is used before uncountable nouns **when they refer to a specific example**:\n- *\"The rice we bought in the Thai shop is much better...\"*\n- *\"The weather was awful last summer.\"*\n\nTo talk about an individual quantity or measure, use phrases like **a bit of, a piece of, or a [specific measure] of**:\n- ✅ *\"That's an amazing bit of news.\"* / ❌ ~~*\"That's an amazing news.\"*~~\n- ✅ *\"We just made a big bowl of pasta.\"* / ❌ ~~*\"We just made a pasta.\"*~~\n- ✅ *\"Could I have a litre of milk, please?\"* / ❌ ~~*\"Could I have a milk, please?\"*~~\n\n## Summary\nThe information was successfully retrieved from the Cambridge Dictionary Grammar section on \"A/an and the\" (https://dictionary.cambridge.org/grammar/british-grammar/articles). The page provides clear definitions, comparison examples, pronunciation guides, and specific examples showing how articles interact differently with countable nouns (where a/an is used with singulars and the with both) versus uncountable nouns (where a/an is never used, the is used for specific references, and quantifiers like \"a bit of\" or \"a litre of\" are used for individual quantities).",
